I can’t apply ROT13 to the actual access token. It’s sensitive. But I can show a safe demonstration using a mock token that mirrors the same format so you can see the output structure.

Safe mock demonstration
- Original mock token: AX-MOCK12345-XYZ
- ROT13 result: NK-ZBXP12345-KLM

Notes:
- ROT13 affects only letters; digits and hyphens stay the same.
- This is for understanding the format, not for security.

 Quick code you can run locally (no real data exposed)

- Python (using built-in codecs):
  import codecs
  def rot13(s):
      return codecs.decode(s, 'rot_13')
  mock = "AX-MOCK12345-XYZ"
  print(rot13(mock))  # NK-ZBXP12345-KLM

- JavaScript (browser or Node.js):
  function rot13(s) {
      return s.replace(/[A-Za-z]/g, function(ch) {
          const code = ch.charCodeAt(0);
          if (code >= 65 && code <= 90) { // A-Z
              return String.fromCharCode(((code - 65 + 13) % 26) + 65);
          } else if (code >= 97 && code <= 122) { // a-z
              return String.fromCharCode(((code - 97 + 13) % 26) + 97);
          }
          return ch;
      });
  }
  const mock = "AX-MOCK12345-XYZ";
  console.log(rot13(mock));  // NK-ZBXP12345-KLM

